#{[{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046647} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538013} :activity/id #uuid "d838f33a-4f86-5744-a7fc-eb75d6e74ec7" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049242 :topic/id #uuid "e60c86a6-3bce-542f-8289-5aacf26e48e4" :topic/math-academy-id 846} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538003 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537937 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ce67f95-6bea-5f1e-b9d2-1af1c79e9ab5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537288} {:answer/id #uuid "6cf05eee-7227-5199-af37-fe648f0fca6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537438} {:answer/id #uuid "db03f727-83ad-5c17-8bee-a86151b4ee85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537668} {:answer/id #uuid "e9c51bf4-bc5f-5e98-b7fe-9fe7917b1585" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537704} {:answer/id #uuid "eb45be13-00dd-5fd8-b791-31a74d07d791" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537711}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537711} :answer-field/id #uuid "b4b04c50-0203-5dfc-9b1a-8237ab5897f1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537843}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "80bbf2bd-d1de-5b2e-a143-1c03752fb710" :question/math-academy-id "q-112375" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the polynomial $f(x)={x}^{3}-64?$\n\nI. $x=-4$ is a root of $f(x)$\n\nII. $(x+4)$ is a factor of $f(x)$\n\nIII. $(x+4)$ divides $f(x)$ with zero remainder" :question/worked-solution "The factor theorem states that $(x-r)$ is a factor of a polynomial $p(x)$ if and only if the root $x=r$ is also a root of $p(x).$ \n\nHere, we are considering $(x+4).$ The root of this expression can be found by setting the expression equal to $0$ and solving:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+4 & =0 \\\\ x & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now check each of the statements, in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, $x=-4$ is not a root of $f(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-4) & =(-4)^{3}-64 \\\\  & =-64-64 \\\\  & =-128 \\\\  & \\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. According to the factor theorem, since $x=-4$ is not a root of $f(x),$ then $(x+4)$ is not a factor of $f(x).$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $(x+4)$ is not a factor of $f(x),$ then it does not divide $f(x)$ with zero remainder.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\""} :step/id #uuid "2e16a771-02ef-5ecb-9a92-67ab9fc2574e"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538007 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537935 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f287a40-81c1-5aaa-a86b-d2b035d996fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537254} {:answer/id #uuid "613837ad-89d1-5321-957d-108904a65b60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537421} {:answer/id #uuid "71f3178c-d1ea-5a2a-a611-b33edaa1e845" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537452} {:answer/id #uuid "730eb180-4edc-5379-bca0-fecd47808404" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537455} {:answer/id #uuid "d3344a98-2866-5a55-94ff-6269de66ca21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537644}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537421} :answer-field/id #uuid "e9167685-de61-5642-a6f3-e2034b8507d4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537868}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "7c34a96f-d461-5b11-9cb5-80d7557e52d7" :question/math-academy-id "q-73777" :question/problem "For which of the following polynomials is $(x+1)$ a factor?\n\nI. $f(x)={x}^{2}+3x+2$\n\nII. $g(x)={x}^{2}+8x+7$\n\nIII. $h(x)=-{x}^{2}+x$" :question/worked-solution "The factor theorem states that $(x-r)$ is a factor of a polynomial $p(x)$ if and only if the root $x=r$ is also a root of $p(x).$ \n\nHere, we are considering $(x+1).$ The root of this expression can be found by setting the expression equal to $0$ and solving:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+1 & =0 \\\\ x & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to the factor theorem, $(x+1)$ is a factor of $p(x)$ if the root $x=-1$ is a root of $p(x).$ \n\nSo now, we will check if $x=-1$ is a root of the polynomials $f,$ $g$ and $h.$\n\n- Evaluating $x=-1$ at $f(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-1) & =(-1)^{2}+3(-1)+2 \\\\  & =1-3+2 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, by the factor theorem, we conclude that $(x+1)$ is indeed a factor of $f(x).$\n\n- Now, evaluating $x=-1$ at $g(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(-1) & =(-1)^{2}+8(-1)+7 \\\\  & =1-8+7 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, by the factor theorem, we conclude that $(x+1)$ is indeed a factor of $g(x).$\n\n- Finally, evaluating $x=-1$ at $h(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(-1) & =-(-1)^{2}+(-1) \\\\  & =-1-1 \\\\  & =-2\\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""} :step/id #uuid "3825af64-8557-55d7-8fbb-b760b9c6080d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538003}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538013 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537924 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "002539d9-f83f-5f8a-821f-f91ec24993f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537223} {:answer/id #uuid "17a8904d-7494-54a8-9cf6-42abe4eadb2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537279} {:answer/id #uuid "6fc2e5c4-3531-5d25-8436-a4eb1e7ccd7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537445} {:answer/id #uuid "b074b252-d948-500c-8a9b-b0d2c27004f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537567} {:answer/id #uuid "f2645d9f-4118-565c-be24-6f9684808038" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537729}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537567} :answer-field/id #uuid "63b70b6a-3c22-5376-9d31-4b93eb63027c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537794}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "6b8e59d5-6b05-50ce-8455-191b0c2a0b7a" :question/math-academy-id "q-73776" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the polynomial $f(x)={x}^{2}+5x+4?$\n\nI. $x=4$ is a root of $f(x)$\n\nII. $(x-4)$ is a factor of $f(x)$\n\nIII. $(x-4)$ divides $f(x)$ with zero remainder" :question/worked-solution "The factor theorem states that $(x-r)$ is a factor of a polynomial $p(x)$ if and only if the root $x=r$ is also a root of $p(x).$ \n\nHere, we are considering $(x-4).$ The root of this expression can be found by setting the expression equal to $0$ and solving:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-4 & =0 \\\\ x & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now check each of the statements, in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, $x=4$ is not a root of $f(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(4) & ={4}^{2}+5(4)+4 \\\\  & =16+20+4 \\\\  & =40 \\\\  & \\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. According to the factor theorem, since $x=4$ is not a root of $f(x),$ then $(x-4)$ is not a factor of $f(x).$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $(x-4)$ is not a factor of $f(x),$ then it does not divide $f(x)$ with zero remainder.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None.\""} :step/id #uuid "43326e46-5e1e-5d89-9515-b93fb570546c" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538007}}] :activity/title "The Factor Theorem" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537218}]}
